## Accessing Vec Elements

Access Vec elements by index using square brackets `[]` or the `.get()` method. Understanding both is essential for safe code.

### Bracket Indexing (Unsafe)

```xiom
let v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(10); v.push(20); v.push(30);

let first = v[0];   // 10
let second = v[1];  // 20
let third = v[2];   // 30
// let bad = v[5];  // RUN-TIME TRAP: index out of bounds
```

Bracket indexing **traps** (panics) if the index is out of bounds.

### Safe Access with .get()

`.get(index)` returns `Option[T]` — `Some(value)` if the index is valid, `None` if out of bounds:

```xiom
let v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(10); v.push(20);

let a = v.get(0);  // Some(10)
let b = v.get(1);  // Some(20)
let c = v.get(5);  // None — safe!

let value = v.get(0).unwrap_or(0);  // safe with default
```

### First and Last Elements

```xiom
let v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(10); v.push(20); v.push(30);

let first = v.get(0);                // Some(10)
let last = v.get(v.len() - 1);       // Some(30)

let empty = Vec[Int]();
let none = empty.get(0);             // None
```

### Modifying by Index

For mutable access, combine with `var` and bracket assignment:

```xiom
var v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(1); v.push(2); v.push(3);

v[0] = 10;  // modify first element
v[1] = 20;  // modify second
// v is now [10, 20, 3]
```

### Bounds Checking

Always check length before accessing by index:

```xiom
fn safe_access(v: &Vec[Int], index: Int) -> Int {
  if index >= 0 && index < v.len() {
    return v[index];
  }
  return -1;
}
```

